## Abstract

Product formulas for Hamiltonian simulation are ubiquitous, easy to implement, but suffer from constants that grow with the number of stages. Multiproduct formulas of Chin, Sheng, and Suzuki linearly combine k-fold applications of a low-order product formula to cancel higher-order error terms, but the standard construction yields coefficients whose one-norm grows exponentially in the target order. We show that choosing the stage counts from Chebyshev-like interpolation points instead yields multiproduct formulas whose coefficient one-norm scales only logarithmically in the target order, giving super-exponentially better conditioning. Combined with oblivious amplitude amplification, this yields a quantum simulation algorithm with cost $O(t\lambda \log^2(t\lambda/\varepsilon))$ that inherits explicit commutator dependence from the base formula.

